# Agent guide

This repository is {{ if .DisplayName }}{{ .DisplayName }}{{ else }}{{ .Container }}{{ end }}, the `{{ .Container }}` {{ if eq .Framework "aws-ec2-ts" }}service of {{ .SoftwareSystem }}: an HTTP
server in TypeScript on EC2 instances in an Auto Scaling group behind an
internal Application Load Balancer{{ else if eq .Framework "aws-fargate-ts" }}service of {{ .SoftwareSystem }}: an HTTP
service in TypeScript on Node, run as a container on Amazon ECS on Fargate
behind an Application Load Balancer{{ else }}function of {{ .SoftwareSystem }}: an AWS
Lambda function in {{ if $go }}Go, on the AWS SDK for Go v2,{{ else }}TypeScript{{ end }} behind an API Gateway HTTP API{{ end }}, keeping its data
in DynamoDB, with its infrastructure as a CDK app in `infra/`, scaffolded by
C4 Factory with the `{{ .Standard }}` Standard. Build it to the rules below. What
it is for, and what it must never do, is in the issue you were given
and the system's needs: follow those.

## The one rule

{{ if $go -}}
`make check` must pass before every commit. It installs the exact packages in
`package-lock.json` and the modules in `src/go.sum`, then checks that
`go mod tidy` changes nothing, runs Prettier and gofumpt (check), ESLint with
`eslint.config.mjs` on the CDK app (typescript-eslint strict, no warnings
allowed), `go vet`, golangci-lint with `.golangci.yml`, `tsc` with the strict
`tsconfig.json`, the function's Go tests (`go test -race`), the stacks'
vitest `Template.fromStack` assertions, `cdk synth` with CDK Nag's AWS Solutions
{{- else -}}
`make check` must pass before every commit. It installs the exact packages in
`package-lock.json`, then runs Prettier (check), ESLint with `eslint.config.mjs`
(typescript-eslint strict, no warnings allowed), `tsc` with the strict
`tsconfig.json`, the vitest tests (the code's and the stacks'
`Template.fromStack` assertions), `cdk synth` with CDK Nag's AWS Solutions
{{- end }}
checks, which fail on any finding not acknowledged with a reason, and
`cfn-lint` on the templates synth wrote. CI runs the same command, and nothing
reaches `main` without it passing and a review. Never commit red.

Nothing here deploys. No command, test or script calls AWS, bootstraps an
account or needs credentials: `cdk synth` writes CloudFormation into
`cdk.out/`, offline.{{ if eq .Framework "aws-fargate-ts" }} No check builds the image either: synth only stages
its build context, and `cdk deploy` builds it, with Docker, on the deployer's
machine.{{ end }}

## Workflow

1. Read the issue, its acceptance criteria, and your role guide:
   `docs/agents/implementer.md` or `docs/agents/reviewer.md`.
2. Read the code you are about to change and match it.
3. Write a failing test first: one per acceptance criterion, or one that shows the bug.
4. Make the smallest change that meets every criterion. One concern per commit.
5. `make check`, then commit with a Conventional Commit message
   (`feat:`, `fix:`, `test:`, `refactor:`, `docs:`).
6. `AGENTS.md`, `docs/agents/` and `.c4factory.yaml` never change. Other protected files
   change only through a Standard change (see your role guide), never in a normal pull
   request. `.devcontainer/`, `.c4factory.yaml` and `.gitea/` belong to the platform.
   `Makefile`, `eslint.config.mjs`, {{ if $go }}`.golangci.yml`, {{ end }}`.prettierrc.json`, `.prettierignore`,
   `.editorconfig`, `AGENTS.md`, `docs/style/`, `docs/agents/`,
   `docs/well-architected.md`, `infra/main.ts` and `infra/standard.ts` belong
   to the Standard. `tsconfig.json`, `vitest.config.mts` and `cdk.json` decide
   what the checks check and which CDK behaviour the app gets: don't loosen them.

## Lints

- Fix every finding; don't silence it. An `eslint-disable-next-line <rule> -- <why>`
  {{- if $go }} or a `//nolint:<linter> // <why>`{{ end }}
  is a last resort for a false positive: one rule, one reason. Never
  `@ts-ignore`; never a file-wide disable.
- Fix every CDK Nag finding in the stack. An acknowledgement
  (`Validations.of(construct).acknowledge({ id, reason })`) is a last resort:
  on the construct the finding's path names, for the one finding (`AwsSolutions-IAM5[Resource::...]`,
  never a whole rule on a stack), with a reason a reviewer can check.
- `make fmt` formats; never hand-format.
{{- if $go }}
- The function is its own Go module in `src/`, apart from `node_modules/`.
  Add a module with `go get` in `src/`, then `go mod tidy`, and commit
  `src/go.mod` and `src/go.sum`. `go build` compiles the function into one
  `bootstrap` binary, so nothing else ships. Prefer Go's standard library and
  the AWS SDK for Go v2's service modules (`github.com/aws/aws-sdk-go-v2/service/*`);
  then few, maintained, well-known modules with a licence that fits. The CDK
  app's packages are `devDependencies` in `package.json`: add one with
  `npm install --save-dev --save-exact` and commit `package-lock.json`.
{{- else }}
- Add packages with `npm install --save-exact` (`--save-dev` for tools), and
  commit `package-lock.json`. esbuild bundles what the {{ if eq .Framework "aws-ec2-ts" }}server{{ else if eq .Framework "aws-fargate-ts" }}service{{ else }}function{{ end }} imports into
  one file, the SDK included, {{ if eq .Framework "aws-fargate-ts" }}in the image's build, {{ end }}so `node_modules` never ships; what it
  imports at run time is a `dependency`, tools and the CDK are
  `devDependencies`. Prefer Node's own modules and the AWS SDK v3's modular
  clients (`@aws-sdk/client-*`, `@aws-sdk/lib-dynamodb`); then few, maintained,
  well-known packages with a licence that fits. A new install script needs an
  `allowScripts` entry in `package.json`: allow it only when the package can't
  work without it.
{{- end }}

## Review rules (lint can't check these)

{{ if $go -}}
1. **Cold start:** the SDK's configuration and clients are created once, in
   `main` in `src/main.go` before `lambda.Start`, and reused by every
   invocation; nothing heavy runs in `init`. The binary imports only the
   service modules it uses.
{{- else if eq .Framework "aws-ec2-ts" -}}
1. **Start-up and shutdown:** SDK clients and configuration are created
   once, in `src/main.ts`, and reused by every request; a missing setting
   stops the server at start-up, not at the first request. On `SIGTERM` the
   server finishes the requests in flight and exits. The bundle imports only
   the clients it uses.
{{- else if eq .Framework "aws-fargate-ts" -}}
1. **Start and stop:** SDK clients and configuration are created once, in
   `src/main.ts`, and reused by every request; nothing heavy runs per
   request that could run once. On `SIGTERM` the server stops taking
   requests and finishes those in flight. The bundle imports only the
   clients it uses.
{{- else -}}
1. **Cold start:** SDK clients and configuration are created once, at module
   scope in `src/handler.ts`, and reused by every invocation; nothing heavy
   runs at import beyond that. The bundle imports only the clients it uses.
{{- end }}
2. **The boundary:** every request is validated before use (path, query,
   body, headers), with unknown input narrowed from `unknown`. Errors are
   answered with the right HTTP status (400 for bad input, 404, 409, 500 for
   a bug) and a JSON message a client can act on, never a stack trace or the
   SDK's error text.
3. **Idempotency:** a retried request or event changes nothing twice. Writes
   are puts of a whole item, conditional writes, or keyed by an idempotency
   key; never a blind increment.
{{ if eq .Framework "aws-ec2-ts" }}4. **Limits are set:** the server caps what it reads (body size) and keeps
   idle connections open longer than the load balancer's 60 seconds; the
   instance type, the Auto Scaling group's minimum and maximum and its scaling
   policy are set in the stack. A request is answered within the load
   balancer's idle timeout; SDK calls stay within it.{{ else if eq .Framework "aws-fargate-ts" }}4. **Limits are set:** the task's CPU and memory and the service's scaling
   bounds are set in the stack for the work; every request body has a
   limit; keep-alive outlasts the load balancer's idle timeout, and the
   container's stop timeout leaves the requests in flight time to finish.{{ else }}4. **Limits are set:** every function has its timeout and memory set in the
   stack, the timeout below API Gateway's 30 seconds and above what the work
   needs; SDK calls stay within it. The stage is throttled.{{ end }}
5. **Logs:** structured JSON, one entry per line, through the {{ if $go }}`*slog.Logger`{{ else }}`Log`{{ end }} the
   code is given; an entry names the request id and what failed. Never
   log a secret, a token or a request body with user data.
6. **Least privilege:** permissions come from CDK grants
   ({{ if eq .Framework "aws-fargate-ts" }}`table.grant(taskDefinition.taskRole, 'dynamodb:GetItem')`{{ else }}`table.grant(fn, 'dynamodb:GetItem')`{{ end }}, `grantReadData`), narrowed to the
   calls the code makes; never an IAM statement with `*` or `service:*`
   actions, never an AWS managed policy on a role.
{{- if $go }}
7. **Errors and context:** every error is handled or returned, wrapped with
   `%w` and what was being done; the invocation's `ctx`, which carries
   Lambda's deadline, goes to every SDK call. No `panic`, `log.Fatal` or
   `os.Exit` past start-up in `main`; no goroutine left running when the
   handler returns. In the CDK app: no `any`, no `as` to silence the
   compiler, exhaustive switches over unions.
8. **Pure code:** the rules live in files that import neither the AWS SDK
   nor `aws-lambda-go`, tested with plain `go test`; one type wraps each AWS
   service behind an interface of the client methods it calls, which tests
   fake; `src/main.go` only wires them.
9. **Tests:** next to the code (`foo_test.go`), table-driven, names that say
   what is expected; no real timers, network or shared state between tests.
   No test calls AWS: SDK clients are faked behind their interface, and
   stacks are checked with `Template.fromStack` assertions.
{{- else }}
7. **Types:** no `any` (narrow `unknown` at the boundary, checked at runtime when
   the input is untrusted); no non-null assertion without a disable comment
   saying why; no `as` to silence the compiler; exhaustive switches over unions.
8. **Pure modules:** logic lives in modules that import neither the AWS SDK
   {{ if eq .Framework "aws-ec2-ts" }}nor `node:http`, tested with plain vitest; one module wraps each AWS
   service behind an interface the tests fake; `src/server.ts` adapts HTTP to
   them and `src/main.ts` only wires them.{{ else if eq .Framework "aws-fargate-ts" }}nor Node's HTTP server, tested with plain vitest; one module wraps each
   AWS service behind an interface the tests fake; `src/main.ts` only
   wires them.{{ else }}nor the `aws-lambda` types, tested with plain vitest; one module wraps each
   AWS service behind an interface the tests fake; `src/handler.ts` only
   wires them.{{ end }}
9. **Tests:** next to the code (`foo.test.ts`), `it.each` tables, names that say
   what is expected; no real timers, network or shared state between tests{{ if eq .Framework "aws-fargate-ts" }};
   no test starts the server{{ end }}.
   No test calls AWS: SDK clients are stubbed behind their interface, and
   stacks are checked with `Template.fromStack` assertions.
{{- end }}
10. **Performance:** when the issue or the system's needs state a budget, a test
    or benchmark measures it on realistic input and the pull request gives the
    numbers.
11. **The stack:** data is encrypted at rest, every log group has a retention,
    every stateful resource an explicit removal policy (`RETAIN` for data), and
    every stack the Standard's tags; `infra/standard.test.ts` checks these. No
    secret in an environment variable or the template: secrets are read from
    Secrets Manager or Parameter Store at run time{{ if eq .Framework "aws-fargate-ts" }}, or passed as ECS `secrets`{{ end }}. Nothing in the app needs
    an AWS account to synthesize: no lookups, no `env` from the environment.
12. **Releases:** user-visible changes get a `CHANGELOG.md` entry under
    "Unreleased"; the version in `package.json` changes only in a release issue.
{{- if $go }}
13. **Names:** Go's: `mixedCaps`,
    initialisms in one case (`ID`, `HTTPURL`), files `snake_case.go`. The CDK
    app's TypeScript: `lowerCamelCase` values, `UpperCamelCase` types,
    `CONSTANT_CASE` module constants, files `snake_case.ts`.
{{- else }}
13. **Names:** `lowerCamelCase` values, `UpperCamelCase` types, `CONSTANT_CASE`
    module constants, acronyms as words (`HttpUrl`); files `snake_case.ts`.
{{- end }}
14. **No dead code:** no commented-out code, speculative options or "for later"
    features; no TODO without an issue number.

